
Web development is the process of building applications that communicate over the internet.

A modern web application usually looks something like:

Browser
    ↓
HTTP/HTTPS
    ↓
Web server
    ↓
Python application
    ↓
Database
    ↓
Response
    ↓
Browser

For example:

User visits:
https://example.com/posts

The browser sends an HTTP request.

The server processes it, potentially queries a database, and sends an HTTP response back.

---

## Web Fundamentals

Before becoming advanced with Django, FastAPI or Flask, you should understand how the web actually works underneath them.

---

## HTTP

HTTP = HyperText Transfer Protocol.

It is the protocol used for communication between clients and web servers.

A client might be:

- Web browser
- Mobile app
- Python program
- JavaScript application
- Another server

A server receives requests and sends responses.

Basic flow:

Client
    ↓
HTTP Request
    ↓
Server
    ↓
HTTP Response
    ↓
Client

---

## HTTP Request

A request tells a server what the client wants.

Example:

GET /users HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com

A request contains things such as:

- Method
- URL/path
- Headers
- Optional body

---

## HTTP Methods

The most important HTTP methods are:

GET
    Retrieve data

POST
    Create/send data

PUT
    Replace/update a resource

PATCH
    Partially update a resource

DELETE
    Delete a resource

HEAD
    Retrieve headers without the response body

OPTIONS
    Ask what operations/options are supported

---

## GET

Used to retrieve data.

Example:

GET /users

Could return:

[
    {"id": 1, "name": "Ben"},
    {"id": 2, "name": "Alice"}
]

GET requests should generally not change server-side data.

---

## POST

Used to create or submit data.

Example:

POST /users

Body:

{
    "name": "Ben",
    "email": "ben@example.com"
}

The server might create a new user.

---

## PUT

Usually used to replace an entire resource.

Example:

PUT /users/1

Body:

{
    "name": "Benjamin",
    "email": "ben@example.com"
}

---

## PATCH

Used to partially update a resource.

Example:

PATCH /users/1

Body:

{
    "name": "Benjamin"
}

Only the specified field needs to change.

---

## DELETE

Deletes a resource.

Example:

DELETE /users/1

---

## HTTP Response

The server sends a response back to the client.

Example:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "name": "Ben"
}

A response contains:

- Status code
- Headers
- Optional body

---

## HTTP Status Codes

Status codes are grouped into categories.

1xx
    Informational

2xx
    Success

3xx
    Redirection

4xx
    Client error

5xx
    Server error

Important examples:

200 OK
    Request succeeded

201 Created
    Resource successfully created

204 No Content
    Successful request with no response body

301 Moved Permanently
    Resource permanently moved

302 Found
    Temporary redirect

304 Not Modified
    Cached version can be used

400 Bad Request
    Invalid request

401 Unauthorized
    Authentication required/failed

403 Forbidden
    Authenticated but not allowed

404 Not Found
    Resource does not exist

405 Method Not Allowed
    HTTP method isn't supported

409 Conflict
    Request conflicts with current state

422 Unprocessable Content
    Request is understood but contains invalid data

429 Too Many Requests
    Rate limit exceeded

500 Internal Server Error
    Server-side error

502 Bad Gateway
    Server received an invalid response from another server

503 Service Unavailable
    Server temporarily unavailable

---

## 401 vs 403

This distinction is extremely important.

401:

"You haven't successfully authenticated."

403:

"You are authenticated, but you aren't allowed to do this."

Example:

User isn't logged in:

GET /my-profile

→ 401

User is logged in but tries to access an admin page:

GET /admin

→ 403

---

## HTTP Headers

Headers provide additional information about requests and responses.

Example:

Content-Type: application/json

Common request headers:

Authorization
    Authentication information

Content-Type
    Format of the request body

Accept
    Formats the client accepts

User-Agent
    Information about the client

Cookie
    Sends cookies to the server

Common response headers:

Content-Type
    Format of the response

Set-Cookie
    Creates/updates cookies

Cache-Control
    Controls caching

Location
    Specifies a redirect/resource location

Access-Control-Allow-Origin
    Controls CORS access

---

## Content-Type

Tells the server/client what format the data uses.

JSON:

Content-Type: application/json

HTML:

Content-Type: text/html

Form data:

Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

File upload:

Content-Type: multipart/form-data

