# APIs

API = Application Programming Interface.

A web API allows software systems to communicate.

Example:

Your application:

GET /api/users

Server:

{
    "users": [
        {
            "id": 1,
            "name": "Ben"
        }
    ]
}

The client can use this data without knowing how the server stores it internally.

---

# REST

REST = Representational State Transfer.

REST is a common architectural style for web APIs.

A REST API generally models resources.

Example:

/users
/users/1
/posts
/posts/42
/comments

---

## REST CRUD

Users:

GET /users
    Get users

POST /users
    Create user

GET /users/1
    Get user 1

PUT /users/1
    Replace user 1

PATCH /users/1
    Update part of user 1

DELETE /users/1
    Delete user 1

This maps HTTP methods to operations on resources.

---

## REST Principles

Important REST ideas include:

- Resources
- Stateless requests
- Standard HTTP methods
- Representations such as JSON
- Clear resource URLs
- Appropriate HTTP status codes

REST APIs should generally avoid relying on server-side conversational state between requests.

---

# JSON APIs

JSON is the most common format for modern web APIs.

Example response:

{
    "id": 1,
    "name": "Ben",
    "email": "ben@example.com"
}

Nested data:

{
    "id": 1,
    "name": "Ben",
    "posts": [
        {
            "id": 10,
            "title": "My First Post"
        },
        {
            "id": 11,
            "title": "Another Post"
        }
    ]
}

---

# API Authentication

Authentication verifies who is making the API request.

Common approaches:

- Session authentication
- API keys
- JWT
- OAuth 2.0
- OpenID Connect

The correct method depends on the application.

---

# API Keys

An API key is a secret/token identifying the client.

Example:

Authorization: Api-Key abc123

or:

X-API-Key: abc123

API keys are commonly used for:

- Server-to-server APIs
- Third-party services
- Developer APIs

Never expose secret API keys in frontend JavaScript.

---

# JWT

JWT = JSON Web Token.

A JWT is a token containing claims that can be cryptographically signed.

Simplified structure:

header.payload.signature

Example concept:

Header:
    Algorithm/type

Payload:
    User information/claims

Signature:
    Used to verify the token

A client might send:

Authorization: Bearer <token>

---

## JWT Flow

Typical flow:

User logs in
    ↓
Server verifies credentials
    ↓
Server creates token
    ↓
Client stores token
    ↓
Client sends token with requests
    ↓
Server validates token
    ↓
Request is accepted/rejected

JWTs are useful, but they are not automatically better than sessions.

---

## JWT Security

Important points:

- Use HTTPS
- Keep token lifetimes appropriate
- Validate signatures
- Validate claims
- Don't put sensitive secrets in the payload
- Think carefully about token storage
- Have a strategy for revocation/expiry

JWT payloads are encoded, not inherently encrypted.

Do not assume:

"JWT = encrypted data."

---

# Authorisation

After authentication, the server needs to determine what the user can do.

Example:

User:
    Ben

Permissions:

Can view posts
Can create posts
Can edit own posts
Cannot edit Alice's posts
Cannot access admin panel

This can be implemented using:

- Permissions
- Roles
- Groups
- Ownership checks
- Scopes

---

# Role-Based Access Control

Example roles:

User
Moderator
Admin

User:

Can create posts

Moderator:

Can remove inappropriate posts

Admin:

Can manage users

This is commonly called RBAC:

Role-Based Access Control

---

# API Pagination

APIs shouldn't necessarily return thousands or millions of records in one response.

Instead:

GET /api/posts?page=1&page_size=20

Response:

{
    "results": [...],
    "page": 1,
    "page_size": 20,
    "total": 500
}

Pagination reduces:

- Response size
- Database load
- Memory usage
- Network usage
- Client processing

---

## Offset Pagination

Example:

/posts?limit=20&offset=40

This means:

Skip 40 records
Return the next 20

Simple and widely used.

---

## Cursor Pagination

Instead of offsets, the API uses a cursor representing a position in the dataset.

Example:

/posts?cursor=abc123

Cursor pagination can perform better for very large or frequently changing datasets.

---

# Rate Limiting

Rate limiting restricts how frequently a client can make requests.

Example:

100 requests per minute

If the limit is exceeded:

429 Too Many Requests

Rate limiting helps prevent:

- Abuse
- Accidental overload
- Brute-force attacks
- Excessive API usage

Common approaches include:

- Requests per second
- Requests per minute
- Token bucket
- Leaky bucket

---

# Webhooks

A webhook allows one system to notify another system when an event occurs.

Without a webhook:

Your application repeatedly asks:

"Did anything happen?"

"Did anything happen?"

"Did anything happen?"

This is polling.

With a webhook:

Event occurs
    ↓
Service sends HTTP request
    ↓
Your server receives it
    ↓
Your server processes the event

Example:

Payment completed
    ↓
Payment provider
    ↓
POST /webhooks/payment
    ↓
Your application

---

## Webhook Security

Never blindly trust incoming webhook requests.

Common protections include:

- Signature verification
- HTTPS
- Secret tokens
- Timestamp validation
- Replay protection
- Idempotency

---

# Idempotency

An operation is idempotent if repeating it produces the same final result.

This is particularly important for APIs and payments.

Imagine:

POST /payments

The request succeeds, but the response gets lost.

The client retries.

Without protection, you might accidentally charge the customer twice.

An idempotency key can prevent duplicate processing.

Example:

Idempotency-Key: abc123

The server remembers that request and avoids processing the same operation twice.

---

# API Versioning

APIs change over time.

Suppose version 1 returns:

{
    "name": "Ben"
}

Later you need to change the format.

Existing applications may depend on the old response.

Versioning allows different API versions to coexist.

Example:

/api/v1/users

/api/v2/users

---

## Versioning Strategies

Common approaches include:

URL:

/api/v1/users

Header:

Accept: application/vnd.example.v1+json

Query parameter:

/users?version=1

URL versioning is simple and commonly used.

---

# API Error Handling

APIs should return useful errors.

Example:

HTTP 400

{
    "error": "Invalid email address"
}

More structured:

{
    "error": {
        "code": "INVALID_EMAIL",
        "message": "The supplied email address is invalid."
    }
}

Clients should be able to understand what went wrong.

---

# API Documentation

A professional API should be documented.

Documentation should explain:

- Endpoints
- HTTP methods
- Parameters
- Request bodies
- Response formats
- Status codes
- Authentication
- Errors
- Rate limits
- Examples

OpenAPI is a common standard for describing APIs.

FastAPI can automatically generate OpenAPI documentation.

---

# CORS

CORS = Cross-Origin Resource Sharing.

Browsers restrict certain cross-origin requests for security reasons.

Example:

Frontend:

https://frontend.example.com

API:

https://api.example.com

These are different origins.

The server can use CORS headers to specify which origins are allowed.

Example:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://frontend.example.com

CORS is primarily a browser security mechanism.

It does not replace API authentication or authorisation.

---

# CSRF

CSRF = Cross-Site Request Forgery.

It involves tricking a user's browser into sending an unwanted authenticated request.

This is particularly relevant to cookie-based authentication.

Django includes built-in CSRF protection.

Important distinction:

CSRF
    Protects against unwanted authenticated browser requests

CORS
    Controls which browser origins may make certain cross-origin requests

They solve different problems.

---

# Web Application Security

You should understand:

- HTTPS
- Authentication
- Authorisation
- Password hashing
- Session security
- CSRF
- XSS
- SQL injection
- CORS
- Rate limiting
- Input validation
- Secure cookies
- Secret management

Never store passwords as plain text.

Use a secure password hashing system provided by a trusted framework/library.

---

# XSS

XSS = Cross-Site Scripting.

An attacker attempts to inject malicious JavaScript into a website.

Example dangerous user input:

<script>
    maliciousCode();
</script>

Applications should properly escape/sanitise untrusted content.

Modern frameworks provide protections, but developers still need to understand how XSS happens.

